WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: fr

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.000
HV11, a monumental work by Nicolas Sanhes

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.200
For 15 years Nicolas Sanhes has produced works
in increasingly important formats.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.400
The control of dimensions and technical constraints
allows him to complete his first public orders

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.600
for the cities of Trappes in 2007
and Montélimar the following year, in France.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:24.000
Built for the city of Valenciennes, HV11 in 2013
will be his most complex work to date.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Today we have the receipt of steel

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.000
raw material to make the sculpture

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.000
of the public order of Valenciennes

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.000
which will hopefully be delivered in June,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
around June 2013.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.000
The project was born a long time ago

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
now it's almost the end, we could say

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.000
because this is the final realization

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.000
it's the construction of the work

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
the project is already sold, it has already been studied.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
It's a project I've been working on for a year and a half.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
It always takes a long time to complete public orders

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.000
because there is the preliminary study, the meeting

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.000
there is site analysis

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.500
then reflection in the workshop to propose a piece for the site

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.500
acceptance of the work by the municipal council

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.500
and then the realization that starts today.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.000
Valenciennes has the distinction of being

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.000
a city whose industrial memory

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.000
was very strong in terms of steel

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.000
and when the city was renovated

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.000
by one of the very famous mayors

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
they decided to conduct a cultural policy

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
very strong to energize the city

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.000
and attract new residents

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.000
and contemporary art has been one of the vectors of this renewal.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.000
So they chose an artistic line

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:36.000
who is the monumental sculpture and the geometric sculpture.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Valenciennes has this policy of acquiring works

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.500
or loan of works

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.000
and it is within this framework through my work

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.500
that I was approached by the deputy mayor

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.500
to propose a project.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:54.000
This is the work that brought me to Valenciennes.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.000
The Valenciennes order is an order that I find great

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.000
because I was invited by the city to come.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.000
The Deputy Mayor for Culture received me on a Sunday afternoon

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.000
we went around the city

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.000
explaining to me all the orders they had placed

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000
it goes from Mark di Suvero to Norman Dilworth

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.000
all great sculptors.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:29.000
And then I was asked to choose a place

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.500
and it was me who, after wandering around, chose a space

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.000
that suited what I wanted to do

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.000
I made the offer to them

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.000
and that was exactly where they wanted me to be.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.500
So finally there is no moment when there is something imposed.

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:45.400
There is really the concern of giving to the artist

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.000
the ability to do what he wants and where he wants.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
And that is very important because this kind of thing is quite rare.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.000
I had no plan. I took into account the physiognomy of the place

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.000
which is a big place

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.000
which acts as both a roundabout

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.500
but that the public can cross

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.500
who is on the city tour

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.000
whose visions are ascending or descending at times

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:46.000
and circular

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.000
so I took into account these physical parameters of space

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.000
to suggest a piece

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.500
but as my work is very complicated, in the monumentality

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.000
because the lines are too complex to be moved

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.500
for example I will have to cut the structure
to take it out of the workshop and put it outside

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.000
so it's very complicated

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:11.000
so I gave myself a lot of time
I asked them 6 months to quietly think about this project

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.000
and give them a definitive plan.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.000
That's what I did.
I worked calmly

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.000
having all the files in front of me
every day in my workshop

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.000
to watch, to soak up

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.000
and things fell into place quietly

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.000
until I get the idea exactly
what I'm looking for Valenciennes.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
After I refined the project,
I reworked it and brought it.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:40.000
747 kilos of flat irons!

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.000
Ok, thank you, see you next time!

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.000
And that's how it starts.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:07.000
If I make a model like this

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.000
it's mainly to understand

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.000
the technical aspects and the resistances of the attachment points

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.000
that the sculpture will undergo

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.000
each line weighing almost a ton

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.000
it will hold only by an assembly

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:25.000
it's almost 800 kilos

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.000
and it's a fiftieth model.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:29.000
There are several angles of view

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:31.000
it's fun to watch today

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.000
how the sculpture will be big

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.000
it is a ratio of scale.

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.000
The public should have this vision.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.500
What I like is to work live

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.000
to be emotional

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:37.000
in this research

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.000
we make art not crafts

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:40.500
otherwise it becomes very complicated

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.000
if we make a small project and multiply it big ...

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.000
This is part of the work theme

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.000
on the rational side of work

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:53.000
but it is also necessary that the feeling, the emotion is there

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:56.000
so that afterwards we can find him in the piece,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.000
that is very important.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.000
I make lines for two or three weeks

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.500
and then I assemble

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:05.500
That's when the magic happens.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.000
The sculpture will be 6 meters long

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.000
almost 6 meters side according to the points of view

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.000
4.5 meters as part of a parallelepiped, and 3 meters high.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.500
It's structural steel beam

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:50.000
200 millimeters, called HEA, which will be fully closed.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.000
Three steel lines that will play like this.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.000
I will have to take it out of the workshop, so I will cut it.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.000
Then we will have the problem of transportation

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.000
so it will definitely be overnight transportation

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:09.000
You have to pass Paris so there are huge constraints.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:13.000
For example, the bridges in the north are lower than in the south.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:17.000
There are a lot of constraints that you have to know too
when we do sculpture

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.000
that's the problem of the transporter

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.500
but it's better to have it in mind

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:25.000
there are these constraints.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.500
Of course you have to make sure that the piece will hold

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.000
there is a ten year guarantee

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.000
there are also safety standards.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:38.000
When I build a piece,

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.500
on the most stretched clothes rails

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.000
I plan an additional charge

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:47.000
in case there are five or six people climbing on it

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:53.000
to celebrate for example a French rugby victory,

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:58.000
you have to take that into account to prevent the sculpture from breaking

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.000
or overturns because that is my responsibility.

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:44.000
These are my assistants

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:49.000
I have one that wears and one that tilts
and then I just have to solder.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.000
Even if it's big, monumental,

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:55.000
it is to the millimeter because

00:12:55.000 --> 00:13:00.000
little by little at the end we can have

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.000
big differences in angle

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.000
and suddenly the piece would not be the same at all.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:19.000
I use the green hoist to climb
and from it to tilt the degrees.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:22.500
It is an awareness of space.

00:13:22.500 --> 00:13:26.000
I am not in a hurry.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:31.000
I'm enjoying this little moment

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:34.000
It allows me to think

00:13:34.000 --> 00:13:40.000
to the whole piece and to work.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:44.000
It is important. It brings a strong connection to make a play like that.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:56.000
We say that we work for a space, a public space, for people ...

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.000
You have to appreciate that moment.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:06.000
And it's just fun.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:10.000
Here and there I am.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:12.000
I use everything when I work

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:17.000
I use my tools of course

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:21.000
my body

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.000
my head of course

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.000
my hands but also my legs

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:28.000
it allows me to adjust

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.000
It holds

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:57.000
You shouldn't be exhausted on the job either

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.000
It always has to be fun

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.000
We must also respond to upcoming exhibitions

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.500
things to show up for

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:12.000
If I start sculpture today
I imagine it takes about three months of hard work

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:13.000
so I prefer at first

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:15.000
since winter is coming and the workshop is not heated

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.000
we will be able to cut steel, it will warm us

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:23.000
and as soon as the beautiful days arrive
we can start finishing and painting

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:29.000
I also manage my work in the workshop
depending on weather conditions!

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.000
I wanted to gain a millimeter ...

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.500
it's done

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.000
Hold left

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
take exit 21bis towards Valenciennes center

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:48.000
Today we are going to make the first location
with technical services, on site

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:51.000
and adjust with the mason the implantation of the sculpture

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.500
In my opinion, this is what we will see with the crane operator earlier

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:07.000
here we will cut traffic during the night

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:09.000
we will put the truck here

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.000
that's what i would do

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:16.500
the truck here, the crane on one side or the other whatever

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:23.500
we take the elements and bring them to the center

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:25.000
or we will put the crane there, and the truck can leave

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:32.000
and then we can work quietly
without stopping the traffic.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:35.500
The approach with Nicolas is original

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:38.500
because it's the first time the city
in the field of geometric abstraction

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:41.000
place an order.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.500
Nicolas is in a way a successor to Bernar Venet

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:49.000
since Bernar Venet who met Nicolas

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:53.000
in Valenciennes which I am very proud of

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:58.000
was very positive about Nicolas' work.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.000
We actually went up in 2011

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.000
with 32 municipalities in the region
a festival which was called "rives, dérives"

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:09.000
along the river Escaut,
in cities crossed by the river

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:13.000
and Nicolas exhibited in a town near Valenciennes

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:16.000
to see his work which attracted me a lot

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:18.000
and then we progressed together.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:22.000
I told him about one of the problems facing me

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:25.500
which was to find a work for one of the city roundabouts

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:30.000
a real work, not what we usually find
on many roundabouts in France.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.000
and he says to me : "I'm going to think"
it was strictly without obligation

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:36.000
and so he thought about it a lot,
it's a complex roundabout

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:38.000
with a lot of roads coming over it

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:41.000
important routes, secondary routes

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:44.000
the city's public park which overlooks this roundabout

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:48.000
and one day he sent me a file
extremely argued and worked

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:54.000
I presented this work to the mayor
and everyone was convinced

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.500
I don't know how it goes in the other cities

00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:00.000
in Valenciennes it's totally subjective

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.500
A work of art is not a brick of soup

00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:06.000
although there can be subjectivity in the soup brick too!

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:11.000
it is a heart stroke for a work and also for the artist

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:13.000
that's how the operations were done

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:19.000
all those that are done in Valenciennes
especially in contemporary art since 2007

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:22.000
with a principle which is geometric abstraction

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:27.500
which is the only caudine fork, with the humanity of the artist

00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:31.000
that is imposed to succeed in doing something in this city.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.000
There is a little handling to do

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:37.000
so I will bring the tackles it takes to properly focus the elements

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:40.000
so unload the truck, bring the central part in the middle on the stud,

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:43.000
the other two lines and we assemble

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.000
It's not very complicated!

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:49.000
Here it is simple, the feeling went well

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:52.000
it will be OK, it will be OK!

00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:01.000
This starts from a historical aspect since
it is the city of Jean-Baptiste Carpeaux.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:04.500
It is the city of France which had the greatest number of Rome prize

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:07.000
more than Paris, about fifty, and a majority of sculptors

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:12.000
So the initial idea was to say that all the sculptors
who had a Rome prize

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.000
be present in their city whether we like them or not

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:19.000
The idea was born that the city is a temporary exhibition gallery

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:24.000
and to propose to artists to deposit a work
for two or three years

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:28.000
we had deposits like that and then we had meetings

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:33.000
and then there are people who are more affordable,
who have been offered to buy

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:43.500
We are in the gardens of the museum of fine arts of Valenciennes

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:48.000
We are next to a work by an English sculptor called Norman Dilworth

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:50.500
a work called "1, 2, 3, 4, 5".

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.000
It's a piece that I really like.

00:20:53.000 --> 00:20:55.000
There are a lot of people who don't go to the museum.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:00.000
I believe that for sculpture there is nothing better than the street.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.000
You just have to walk through the streets of the city to see
how there are starting to be works everywhere

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:09.000
We will continue to develop it,
replace those who leave

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:15.000
and also acquire it because artists are never so important
that when they are alive

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:22.000
it's good to participate in the development of their career
and allow them to live simply

00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.000
I am in front of the "grande blanche"

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:29.000
It's a loan to the city that I installed in September

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:32.500
which I will remove when I have installed the sculpture in Place Cardon.

00:21:32.500 --> 00:21:35.500
It's a loan.

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:38.000
It is so that the public of Valenciennes

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:43.000
get a little familiar with the geometry of my work.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:50.000
It must have been pretty like that in white

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:53.000
snow, white sculpture ...

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.000
It will need a little cleaning in the spring

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:04.000
this is normal, white is more messy than raw steel.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:10.000
I'm glad it is there
it works both in winter and summer.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.000
in summer it is really covered by the big trees which dominate it

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.000
and that closes the space

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:19.000
and the location with the architecture of the museum suits it well.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:24.000
It is a loan that I am glad to have made.

00:22:26.000 --> 00:22:31.000
On the deposit of the "grande blanche"
the feedback is really very favorable.

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.000
There are always people who don't like

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:36.000
Fortunately! I used to say
that unanimity scares me very much in this matter

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:39.500
I prefer people to oppose it,
find it not okay at all and argue

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:43.000
rather than indifference.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:46.000
Here I really feel that it is going well

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:48.000
There are always people to tell you that it's very good

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:51.000
We're not completely blind or fooled but

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.000
I have my antennas, my network,
which tells me that people generally like it

00:22:55.000 --> 00:22:59.500
and there's an impatience to see the next one arrive.

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:04.000
Nicolas will improve the lives of Valenciennes for eternity

00:23:04.000 --> 00:23:07.000
finally our eternity since it is obvious

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:13.000
that HV11 is going to have a higher eternity
to Nicolas and to me, I believe.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:20.000
The idea of ​​this temporary installation is to see

00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:26.000
the amplitude that the center line of the sculpture
occupies in portal space

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:33.000
to calculate exactly the cuts I will have to make
on the final piece.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:38.000
The latitude that I have between the sides of the portal
and the line is 20 centimeters.

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:42.000
That means that when I'm going to assemble the structure definitively

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:48.000
I will have to stand above this line of 20 centimeters maximum
and there I can go out

00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:51.000
if I exceed I will have to break the gate.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:19.000
The cut I'm going to make here must be equal to the one above

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:22.000
in this part there and in that one.

00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:29.500
after when i position the piece
i'm sure to fall wherever i want

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:32.000
and we see whether to remove material or add more

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.000
for example there I need to add 3 centimeters

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:38.000
3 centimeters is missing ... it happens!

00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:03.000
I'm trying to set up the plate
which will hold the two lines together

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:08.000
It will be completely in space.

00:27:08.000 --> 00:27:11.000
It must hold!

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:33.000
I know that this central part

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:35.000
it is the body of the sculpture.

00:27:35.000 --> 00:27:41.000
All the tension will end up here with the third line
which I will position next week.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:54.500
By putting the bolts I increase the rigidity

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:56.500
These are bolts that support 3 tonnes each

00:27:56.500 --> 00:28:02.000
So here we are on 12 tonnes of pressure
so it risks absolutely nothing.

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:13.000
The work is done empirically

00:28:13.000 --> 00:28:15.000
but I'm starting to control the material

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.000
I know where to make it more solid

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:21.000
when I work on assemblies.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.000
but there is always a tension

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:26.000
When we do the piece, the distance is missing

00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:30.000
which is acquired with the maturity of the work

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:37.000
After this piece I can go even further
in the work, the design of the pieces

00:28:37.000 --> 00:28:42.000
I can now do removable things
so far this has not been the case.

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:46.000
see even bigger

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:49.000
I can make it twice as big

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:55.000
To give an idea it's 12 meters long and 6 meters high

00:28:55.000 --> 00:29:00.000
which is roughly the height of the workshop volume

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.000
and the beam 400 by 400

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:06.000
it makes squares like that

00:29:07.000 --> 00:29:10.000
It's not to be megalomaniac

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:13.000
is to go further in sculptural research

00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:17.000
to stretch these lines in space

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:21.000
and relate sculpture to architecture

00:29:21.000 --> 00:29:27.000
because here the sculpture looks big
but in the urban space it will be smaller

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:29.000
but it is sized in relation to the space that will accommodate it

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:33.000
if I put it next to a building in Shanghai it is ridiculous

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:37.000
We must also have this awareness that in work

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:41.000
you have to be able to develop in the long term

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:46.000
it goes through workshop work,
by solving technical problems.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.000
It's always a challenge, a victory over yourself

00:29:53.000 --> 00:29:57.000
when I spend weeks or months alone in my workshop

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:59.000
I learn something even if I am alone

00:29:59.000 --> 00:30:03.000
It's pushing your limits and also bringing dreams

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.000
to myself and to those who will look at the sculpture

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:09.000
saying to himself : "how does it hold?"

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:15.000
It is this notion of freedom that is important

00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:20.000
and it is also to say :
"no i don't let myself be dictated by the material!"

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:26.000
My sculpture is a formal matter

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:30.000
so I have to bring the material to my intention
it's not the opposite!

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:46.000
Material has limits
but we can overcome the limits.

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:54.000
By looking at how the material plays
where it spins, where it works

00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:58.000
There are zones where it works more than others

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:04.000
it is by observing

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:09.000
we realize if it twists, if it works

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:11.000
so we strengthen

00:31:11.000 --> 00:31:15.000
It's engineering work that also
part of the sculptor's work

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:20.000
Technicality is important in artistic work

00:31:23.000 --> 00:31:27.000
maybe some contemporary artists forget it

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:44.500
When I started making the pieces of wood

00:31:44.500 --> 00:31:47.500
I made them here or I worked outside

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:54.000
This is where the first tensions occured

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:01.000
then I developed them in a more emancipated way
in the large workshop

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:04.500
This is where it started

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:10.000
I use it for all the management of my work

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:15.000
drawing and recently painting

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:21.000
the painting came at the same time
that Valenciennes sculpture

00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:31.000
It was a need to keep an intellectual freshness
in the hard work of the workshop

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:35.000
When I started HV11 I had a plan on this table

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:37.000
with photos of the site

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:41.500
after I made a file

00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:47.500
The idea is to wonder how we apprehend this place

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:51.000
how we get there, how we live it and how we get out of it.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:56.000
These are circular movements due to its shape

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:59.000
it's almost an octagon

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:05.000
The idea is that we also have in sculpture this movement

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:10.000
that I managed to give thanks
at the inclination of the lines in space.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:20.000
and that the motorist or the pedestrian goes around the place
have this revolving vision

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:22.500
The computer is a tool just like the welding machine

00:33:22.500 --> 00:33:28.000
When I work on a project
I use the photo a lot, the work of the workshop

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:35.000
then I make computer generated images

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:38.000
so that sponsors have a vision

00:33:38.000 --> 00:33:41.500
very precise of the final rendering

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:47.000
This is important, it allows me
to adjust the size of the piece

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:53.000
We're going to have a big white rectangle
on which the sculpture will be placed.

00:33:53.000 --> 00:34:00.000
When I'm in the workshop I feel good, I am out of time.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:05.000
Even in contemporary art there are many artists

00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:15.000
who have an approach where the works are too imprinted
of the world we live in

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:18.000
too loaded with technology

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:21.000
I try to be out of it

00:34:21.000 --> 00:34:26.000
Distance seem necessary to me
to understand the world we are in.

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:58.000
It's 5 months of work

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.000
with very different phases

00:35:06.000 --> 00:35:10.000
phases of concern over the completion of the project

00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:16.000
of combat, of duration in time

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:22.000
to keep the intellectual freshness of artistic work

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:27.000
in the complexity of the work
and the physical difficulty of the work.

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:44.000
This sculpture represents a certain passage

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:52.000
it has a different conception of space
than the previous pieces

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:56.000
it was complex to maintain the mass of the sculpture

00:35:56.000 --> 00:36:01.000
because some lines weigh more than 1.5 tonnes

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:06.000
it was a major technical challenge

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:12.000
which makes me think that I can go even further

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:17.000
in the tension of the lines and the design of the sculpture.

00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:24.000
It is a passage which opens on another stage

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:28.000
higher, more ambitious, just for fun

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:37.000
to go further in research,
in the stake, in the risk taking

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:41.000
which is important to me if we want to move forward in life.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:48.000
I leave time to time

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:52.000
if things have to come it's good,
otherwise it doesn't matter

00:36:52.000 --> 00:36:57.000
What is important is that every day that I am there
I take pleasure in doing this.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:56.000
No, no, wait, we're not going!

00:38:14.000 --> 00:38:16.000
I'm going to push with Eric

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:36.000
It is not the largest.

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:38.000
The largest to date is that
of Montélimar which is 7 meters high

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:41.000
This one is more complex

00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:47.000
than that of Montélimar which has more open lines

00:38:47.000 --> 00:38:51.000
Here they are more closed so we increase the weight

00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:54.000
The spatial design is not the same.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:56.000
It is the most complex.

00:38:56.000 --> 00:39:02.000
This work was important
because it allowed me to open the sculpture

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:09.000
My previous works are smaller.
The welds were sufficient

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:12.000
so that the lines hold.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:17.000
When we have more range, we know that steel
is flexible, it bends

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:20.000
it is therefore necessary to reinforce in specific zones

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:23.000
so that it does not break later.

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:39.500
I'm happy with this piece,

00:39:39.500 --> 00:39:42.500
it lets me predict an opening
greater in my work

00:39:42.500 --> 00:39:45.000
and greater risk-taking

00:39:45.000 --> 00:39:48.000
There are no limits, except those we set for ourselves.

00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:51.000
I don't give myself limits.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:39.000
It's not a heartbreak at all,
it's even a certain pleasure

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:42.000
to share it with the public

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:45.000
I'm waiting for this pretty nice moment

00:40:45.000 --> 00:40:50.000
which will fill 5 months of solitude in the workshop

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:53.000
and I have other projects pending

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:57.000
I can't wait to start another piece

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:04.000
to take further risk-taking

00:41:04.000 --> 00:41:07.000
of the arrangement of lines in space.

00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:15.000
You have to think that the sculpture
is made for a specific site

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:19.000
initially it has a circular design

00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:24.000
you get the impression of movement
when you discover the sculpture

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:28.000
as if we were being told a story
going around the piece.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:03.000
We create the piece according to the place

00:42:03.000 --> 00:42:06.000
precisely so that it is just in relation to space.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:09.000
It shouldn't be too big

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:12.000
Do not impose things

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:18.500
I'm here to do the fairest thing possible.

00:42:18.500 --> 00:42:22.000
There you will need a small chamfer

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:26.000
The solder point allows me to adjust the top element.

00:42:26.000 --> 00:42:29.000
This will make a benchmark.

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:32.000
So we'll put a punch.

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:33.500
No problem.

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:36.000
Thank you!

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:41.500
We would have to put two straps

00:42:41.500 --> 00:42:44.000
that we pass on each side. We raise ...

00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:50.000
Then we put it there.

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:07.500
I saw the place, I will have the answer when the sculpture is placed

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:13.500
I will see if I have captured the space

00:43:13.500 --> 00:43:20.500
if the device of its positioning in the space of the roundabout

00:43:20.500 --> 00:43:25.000
is fair compared to the visions I had of sculpture

00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:28.000
All of this was imagined from a plan

00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:32.000
from computer generated images

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:39.000
I hope my feeling of the place will be right.

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:43.500
I will see if it is fair in its dimensions

00:43:43.500 --> 00:43:46.500
Maybe it will be a little small or its real size

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:54.000
Next time we will try to do it differently

00:43:54.000 --> 00:43:57.000
but i think it will be good

00:44:01.500 --> 00:44:03.000
Its good ?

00:44:17.000 --> 00:44:20.000
Its good ? It bolts ... great!

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:26.000
Now we put the other part

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:29.000
then we adjust well relative to the center

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:31.500
we adjust the concrete plates well

00:44:31.500 --> 00:44:34.000
then we weld and it's over.

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:51.500
We are on schedule so we have time.

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:56.000
You have to lower!

00:45:03.500 --> 00:45:06.000
What is important is the relationship that there can be

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:10.000
between the lines, and their spatial arrangements

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:14.000
They must be close enough

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:18.000
not too far apart otherwise the sculpture lacks coherence

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:21.500
it's trying to find a happy medium.

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:24.500
The lines get married, they tie together on points

00:45:24.500 --> 00:45:30.000
these are fragile points
it just rests on angles.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:36.000
The idea is that so that things
where human beings can exist

00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:40.000
in any case live normally

00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:43.000
or at least with a notion of balance

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:48.000
it is this distance that we put between each other
so that things are fair.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:51.000
I don't invent anything, I just look at the world

00:45:51.000 --> 00:45:54.500
and I try to do with my feelings, with my story

00:45:54.500 --> 00:46:00.000
to put these ideas into place in a formal proposal.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:05.000
In daily work a logical sequence is established

00:46:05.000 --> 00:46:07.000
the concepts are in place

00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:10.000
as well as a certain idea of ​​geometry.

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:16.500
This is the result of simple observations,
daily, over time

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:20.500
that make things valuable,
an existence, a thickness

00:46:20.500 --> 00:46:23.500
and develop afterwards.

00:46:26.000 --> 00:46:28.000
Yes, above and below ...

00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:43.000
If tonight I can put them down it's perfect

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:47.500
after tomorrow i paint
and I would see for the gravel

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:51.500
I have the crane for another two hours
so we will not hurry, the check is the same!

00:46:51.500 --> 00:46:54.000
I have the big grinder it will go faster, I will make it!

00:46:55.000 --> 00:46:57.500
Make the big one I will finish

00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:01.500
We remove!

00:47:02.000 --> 00:47:03.500
Are we removing or lifting?

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:06.000
We remove, we remove!

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:08.000
We dismantle!

00:47:13.500 --> 00:47:16.500
Two more hours of work to finish the turntables

00:47:16.500 --> 00:47:21.000
we will have to work hard on it.

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:23.500
I will resin tomorrow if necessary.

00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:30.500
Philippe thank you very much! See you next time.

00:47:32.000 --> 00:47:34.000
Eric thank you!

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:23.000
We're going to lift it, ok?

00:48:30.500 --> 00:48:33.500
It says "HV11", then he will explain to you what it is

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:43.000
"H" is the first letter of the material, "HEA"

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:46.500
"V" is the initial of Valenciennes

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:50.000
"11" is the year of meeting

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:51.500
It's really simple!

00:48:51.500 --> 00:48:54.000
We see those who have read the text or not!

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:57.500
We understood why, that's it!

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:08.000
Did you design it according to space?

00:49:08.000 --> 00:49:10.000
Yes

00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:14.000
Of course

00:49:14.000 --> 00:49:16.500
You have to understand the place

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:19.000
You have to feel it.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:22.000
It is designed to guide the movement of the square.

00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:29.500
In sculpture it has to hold,
it is the characteristic of sculpture

00:49:29.500 --> 00:49:34.500
but it has to hold physically and plastically.

00:49:35.500 --> 00:49:37.000
So there is a double issue like that.

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:41.000
It is very important to be able to link these two aspects

00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:46.000
without it being immediately noticeable.

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:52.000
Line adjustment,
this relationship we can have with the piece

00:49:53.000 --> 00:49:58.000
this emotion that we can have
because we don't really know how it holds.

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:01.500
It's the magic of sculpture

00:50:01.500 --> 00:50:04.500
to have a shape like that in space.

